---
/**
 * /work-with-me — P08 §3.2, verbatim.
 *
 * Two doors, one page, never a shared form and never a shared scan-path.
 * §3.3(b), the price-adjacency rule: the $2,400 Sprint price must never sit in
 * the same uninterrupted reading path as a Door 1 build price. Compliance here
 * is structural — the page forks in its opening two lines, and Door 1
 * terminates in an explicit "you are done" before section 2's heading.
 * Bracketed items in the spec are gates in ../data/profile.ts and do not
 * render until answered (§2.2: "Do not publish a bracket").
 */
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import {
    calendlySprint15,
    calendlySpecTools20,
    contractCommitments,
    deskWindow,
    email,
    paymentRail,
    referenceBullet,
    sprintSlots,
} from '../data/profile';

const opamedia = (path: string, campaign: string) =>
    `https://opamediatech.com${path}?utm_source=lekeabiodun&utm_medium=referral&utm_campaign=${campaign}`;

const body = 'prose prose-zinc max-w-none dark:prose-invert';
const offer =
    'not-prose my-6 rounded-lg border border-gray-300 bg-gray-50 p-6 dark:border-gray-700 dark:bg-gray-800/50';
const offerTitle = 'font-medium text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100';
const offerBody = 'mt-3 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300';
---

<Layout
    title="Work with me"
    description="Two ways to work together: a specifier journey teardown for manufacturers whose product is hard to specify, and a fixed-price production stability sprint for teams carrying a bug backlog."
>
    <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-2xl px-4 py-12">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 sm:text-4xl">
            Work with me
        </h1>

        <div class={`${body} mt-6`}>
            <p>
                There are two ways to do this, and they are genuinely different. Pick the one that
                describes you and skip the other. If you're a manufacturer, everything you need is in
                section 1 and it ends with a link out — you never need to read section 2.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- ══ DOOR 1 ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="mt-14 border-t-4 border-gray-900 pt-8 dark:border-gray-100">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
                1. You make a complicated product and buying it is harder than it should be
            </h2>

            <div class={`${body} mt-6`}>
                <p>
                    You manufacture something that gets specified rather than purchased — lighting,
                    ceilings, acoustics, glazing, casework, commercial furniture. It comes in sizes ×
                    finishes × mountings × performance options. Somewhere between "an architect is
                    interested" and "a purchase order exists" there is a human being reading options off a
                    PDF, and that human is your bottleneck.
                </p>
                <p>The first step is not a build. It's a teardown.</p>
            </div>

            <div class={offer}>
                <p class={offerTitle}>Specifier Journey Teardown — $1,950, seven business days</p>
                <div class={offerBody}>
                    <p>
                        I walk your product the way an architect would, from the moment they land to the
                        moment they'd have a price. I count the exact drop-out points. You get the
                        walkthrough, the screenshots, the ranked fix list, and a signable fixed-price build
                        scope with a named start date attached to it.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If it isn't useful, tell me within five business days and I refund it in full — no
                        reason required, and you keep the document. If you go on to book a build within 60
                        days, the $1,950 comes off.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={body}><p>Then, if the findings warrant it:</p></div>

            <div class={offer}>
                <p class={offerTitle}>Specification Configurator — Starter — $12,500, six weeks</p>
                <div class={offerBody}>
                    <p>
                        One product family, rules engine, real catalogue number and spec sheet out the other
                        end, quote request into your inbox.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={offer}>
                <p class={offerTitle}>Specification Configurator — Standard — $28,000, ten weeks</p>
                <div class={offerBody}>
                    <p>
                        Full product line, complex option logic, spec-sheet output, CRM or email handoff.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={body}>
                <p>No licence fee. No per-model charge. You own it.</p>
                <p>
                    For comparison, because I'd want the comparison: the platform alternatives publish their
                    own list prices in euros — €15,000–€50,000+ per year in licence alone, plus €1,200–€2,000
                    per product model to set up. Those are itqlick's configurator pricing survey and CPQ3D's
                    2026 pricing survey, and I'll send you both links. If you have 40 SKUs, the model setup
                    fees alone come to €48,000–€80,000 — more than this entire project. Ask me on the call
                    and I'll convert it to dollars at that day's ECB reference rate.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <a href={opamedia('/tacet-acoustic-lighting-configurator-case-study', 'tacet-casestudy')}>
                        See what this looks like: the Tacet configurator, built for Axis Lighting &rarr;
                    </a>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <a href={opamedia('/product-configurators', 'work-with-me-door1')}>
                        Start here: opamediatech.com/product-configurators &rarr;
                    </a>
                </p>

                {calendlySpecTools20 && (
                    <p>
                        Or book 20 minutes with me directly: <a href={calendlySpecTools20}>book a call &rarr;</a>
                        <br />
                        No deck. I'll ask you how many valid configurations the product has and how a
                        specifier gets a price today. If the answer is "they call a rep," we have something
                        to talk about. If it isn't, I'll tell you that on the call and we'll both keep our
                        afternoon.
                    </p>
                )}

                <p>
                    This work is delivered by my company, Opamedia. Written scope, milestone payments, and a
                    case study you can read before you speak to anyone.
                </p>
            </div>

            {/* §3.3(c) — the explicit terminator. Door 1 ends before any Door 2 price is readable. */}
            <div class="my-12 border-y border-gray-200 py-6 text-center dark:border-gray-800">
                <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
                    — that is the end of section 1 —
                </p>
                <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-sm text-gray-500">
                    If you are a manufacturer, you are done. Section 2 is a different service for a
                    different buyer and the prices in it have nothing to do with the prices above.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ══ DOOR 2 ══════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="border-t-4 border-gray-900 pt-8 dark:border-gray-100">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
                2. Something is broken in production and it has been broken for a while
            </h2>

            <div class={`${body} mt-6`}>
                <p>
                    You have a system carrying real users and real money. There is a list of things that go
                    wrong. Nobody has had a clear week to sit with it, and every week it costs a little more.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class={offer}>
                <p class={offerTitle}>Production Stability Sprint — $2,400, five business days</p>
                <div class={offerBody}>
                    <p>
                        Up to five named production bugs: reproduced, root-caused, fixed, tested, shipped.
                        Plus a written fragility map — the parts of the system most likely to be next, and
                        why — and a runbook for everything I touched.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={body}>
                <p>That is the whole offer. There is no hourly version of it.</p>

                <p>How the five bugs actually work, so nobody is surprised on day five:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>You name up to five before we start, and they go in the scope document by name.</li>
                    <li>
                        Day one is reading. I don't bill for it separately — it's inside the five days and
                        inside the $2,400.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        By 17:00 my time on day two you get a written triage: which ones I have reproduced,
                        which I have not, and the list I am committing to for days three to five. That
                        committed list is what "done" means.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        A bug I cannot reproduce by end of day two does not get abandoned quietly — it gets
                        written up with everything I tried, and you can swap in another from your backlog at
                        no charge.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        I do not run into a sixth day. At 17:00 on day three I check two things: is every
                        committed bug root-caused — cause named in a sentence, not just reproduced — and have
                        I used more than ten of the sixteen hours. If either answer is bad, you get an email
                        that evening telling you so, with what the rest would actually take. You decide. I
                        don't absorb it and go quiet, and I don't bill you for extra without asking.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <p>
                    Start dates are Mondays and Thursdays only. That is not a scheduling quirk — it is how I
                    make sure two sprints never land on the same five days, which is the only way the fifth
                    day of yours still gets my full attention.
                </p>

                <p>What I'm actually good at:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        Going into a codebase somebody else wrote and making it behave — Laravel, React,
                        Next.js, Python
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Bug backlogs, edge cases, the thing that only happens in production on a Tuesday
                    </li>
                    <li>Technical debt with a deadline attached</li>
                    <li>Complex product and configuration logic, and the front end that carries it</li>
                    <li>Being the one who reads the whole thing before touching it</li>
                </ul>

                <p>How it works:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>I take two of these at a time, never starting on the same day.</li>
                    {deskWindow && (
                        <li>
                            {deskWindow} every weekday. That covers the whole US Eastern and Central morning,
                            all year: Nigeria has no daylight saving, so when your clocks change in November
                            and March, mine doesn't, and the overlap holds either way.
                        </li>
                    )}
                    <li>
                        A written paragraph in your channel every day, whether or not there is good news. If
                        you have ever had a contractor go quiet for three days, this is the part of the offer
                        that matters.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        Your NDA, unsigned by me, is fine. Send it, I'll sign it same day. I don't have a
                        competing one.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        If you're an agency: I work behind your logo, on your repo, under your NDA, and I
                        will never contact your client. Ever. Not after the project, not in two years. Put it
                        in writing and I'll sign that too.
                    </li>
                </ul>

                <p>
                    And the same guarantee as every other first engagement: if you ask within five business
                    days of delivery, you get the full $2,400 back, you keep everything I produced, and I
                    don't ask why.
                </p>

                <p>If it doesn't stop after the sprint, there's a retainer:</p>
            </div>

            <div class={offer}>
                <p class={offerTitle}>Production Stability Retainer — $4,500/month</p>
                <div class={offerBody}>
                    <p>
                        Up to 25 hours a month, four-business-hour response on production issues, weekly
                        triage call. I take one of these at a time.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class={body}>
                {sprintSlots && <p>{sprintSlots}</p>}

                {calendlySprint15 && (
                    <p><a href={calendlySprint15}>Book 15 minutes &rarr;</a></p>
                )}
                {email && (
                    <p>
                        Or just send me the repo and the five bugs:{' '}
                        <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>
                    </p>
                )}
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ══ FAQ ═════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
        <section class="mt-14 border-t-4 border-gray-900 pt-8 dark:border-gray-100">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100">
                Before you decide — the things you're allowed to ask
            </h2>

            <div class={`${body} mt-6`}>
                <h3>Where are you?</h3>
                <p>
                    Lagos, Nigeria. WAT, UTC+1, no daylight saving — so the offset to you changes twice a
                    year and never on my end.
                </p>

                {/* §3.6 gate — nothing about payment publishes until the $1 ACH test has passed. */}
                {paymentRail && (
                    <>
                        <h3>How do I pay you, and is this going to be complicated?</h3>
                        <p>
                            No. Here is the whole thing in advance so you don't have to ask your finance
                            person first.
                        </p>
                        <ul>
                            <li>You are invoiced by {paymentRail.entity}, in US dollars.</li>
                            {paymentRail.accountLine && (
                                <li>
                                    Payment goes to a US-domiciled account in my name with a routing number
                                    and an account number — {paymentRail.accountLine}. Your AP system treats
                                    it as a domestic ACH transfer. No SWIFT, no correspondent-bank fees, no
                                    international wire form.
                                </li>
                            )}
                            <li>
                                I hold a completed {paymentRail.w8Form} and I will send it with the first
                                invoice without being asked.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                All the work is performed in Nigeria, so it is foreign-source income under US
                                rules. There is nothing for you to withhold and no 1099 to issue. If your
                                finance team wants that in writing before you raise a PO, ask and I'll put it
                                in an email.
                            </li>
                            <li>
                                Terms: 50% on signature, 50% on delivery for the Sprint. Net 15. Milestones
                                for anything larger.
                            </li>
                            {paymentRail.coiMsa && <li>{paymentRail.coiMsa}</li>}
                        </ul>
                    </>
                )}

                {/* §2.7.1 — publish only the lines that are actually contracted. */}
                {contractCommitments && (
                    <>
                        <h3>What if you disappear?</h3>
                        <p>
                            Every repository is in your organisation from the first commit. Every project
                            ships with a written runbook — six sections, listed on the scope document, written
                            on day one rather than day five. Milestone payments mean you are never more than
                            one milestone exposed.
                        </p>
                        {referenceBullet && (
                            <p>
                                And I'll give you a client to call before you commit; I don't script them and
                                I'm not on the call.
                            </p>
                        )}
                    </>
                )}

                <h3>Do you do hourly staff augmentation?</h3>
                <p>
                    Not under my own name, and not open-ended. My company, Opamedia, does outcome-scoped
                    augmentation with a defined deliverable and a minimum engagement — if that's what you
                    want, say so on the first call and I'll tell you in one minute whether it fits. If what
                    you actually need is a body for N hours a week with no defined outcome, I'm the wrong
                    person and I'd rather say that now.
                </p>

                <h3>Do you do a three-month retainer?</h3>
                <p>
                    Yes, in one shape: the Production Stability Retainer at $4,500 a month, up to 25 hours,
                    four-business-hour response on production issues. I take one at a time — so sometimes the
                    honest answer is "yes, from next month."
                </p>

                <h3>Do you do a discovery phase?</h3>
                <p>
                    Yes, and it has a price rather than being folded into a proposal: the $1,950 Specifier
                    Journey Teardown. Seven business days, refundable for five, and credited in full against
                    a build booked within 60 days.
                </p>

                <h3>What don't you do?</h3>
                <p>
                    Greenfield product builds from a blank page. Design-only work. Anything under $1,950.
                    Projects where nothing at all can ever be published in any form, including anonymised.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>
    </div>
</Layout>
